window.docContents[49].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Quantifiers___-Implications___-Functions":{"contents":"In Lean's computational universe (where data structures and\nprograms live), there are two sorts of values that have arrows in\ntheir types: constructors introduced by inductively defined\ndata types, and functions.\n\nSimilarly, in Lean's logical universe (where we carry out proofs),\nthere are two ways of giving evidence for an implication:\nconstructors introduced by inductively defined propositions,\nand... functions!\n\n\n\nFor example, consider this statement:\n\ntheorem even_plus4 (n : Nat) (h : Even n) :\n    Even (n + 4) := by\n  apply Even.succ_succ\n  apply Even.succ_succ\n  apply h\n\n\nWhat is the proof object corresponding to even_plus4?\n\n\n\nWe're looking for an expression whose type is ∀ (n : Nat), Even n → Even (n + 4) — that is, a function that takes two arguments (one\nnumber and a piece of evidence) and returns a piece of evidence!\n\nHere it is:\n\ntheorem even_plus4' : ∀ (n : Nat), Even n → Even (n + 4) :=\n  fun (n : Nat) => fun (h : Even n) =>\n    Even.succ_succ (n + 2) (Even.succ_succ n h)\n\n\nRecall that fun n => blah means \"the function that, given n,\nyields blah,\" and that Lean treats n + 4 and n + 2 + 2\nas synonyms. Another equivalent way to write this definition is:\n\n\n\ntheorem even_plus4'' (n : Nat) (h : Even n) :\n    Even (n + 4) :=\n  Even.succ_succ (n + 2) (Even.succ_succ n h)\n\n#check (even_plus4'' : ∀ (n : Nat), Even n → Even (n + 4))\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence","header":"10.2. Quantifiers, Implications, Functions","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Quantifiers___-Implications___-Functions"}});